  "summary": "An internal actor pivoted against the file server we9041srv, first probing it anonymously over SMB named pipes (lsarpc/samr) for account and policy information, then switching to authenticated Administrator access from the same source to open service-control and registry pipes -- the classic setup for remote service execution -- while a second internal host ran a much larger automated anonymous-logon enumeration sweep against the same RPC pipes; taken together this looks like reconnaissance and preparation for further lateral movement or credential harvesting using an already-compromised Administrator account, though IP reputation checks on both source addresses returned no external threat data (both are internal RFC1918 hosts), so attribution beyond 'internal actor with valid admin credentials' cannot be established from this evidence alone.",
  "hosts_involved": [
   "we9041srv.waynecorpinc.local",
   "192.168.2.50",
   "192.168.250.100",
   "WE8105DESK$"
  ]
 },
 "verdict": {
  "headline": "An internal host at 192.168.2.50 escalated from an anonymous SMB null session to an authenticated Administrator session on the Wayne Corp file server within two seconds and then opened service-control and registry named pipes, while a second internal host (192.168.250.100) ran a large automated anonymous SMB enumeration sweep against the same server -- a pattern consistent with credential-abuse reconnaissance and lateral-movement staging, not yet confirmed as external compromise.",
  "claims": [
   {
    "text": "An anonymous SMB session and an authenticated Administrator SMB session, both from the same source IP 192.168.2.50, hit the file server's lsarpc pipe within two seconds of each other.",
    "support": "supporting",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 2,
      "quoted_span": "NT AUTHORITY\\ANONYMOUS LOGON"
     },
     {
      "finding_index": 3,
      "quoted_span": "WAYNECORPINC\\Administrator"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "text": "That same Administrator session from 192.168.2.50 went on to open the svcctl (service control manager) and winreg (remote registry) named pipes minutes later, a pipe combination associated with remote service creation as used by tools like PsExec.",
    "support": "supporting",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 7,
      "quoted_span": "RecordNumber=1083476"
     },
     {
      "finding_index": 8,
      "quoted_span": "RecordNumber=1083562"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "text": "A second internal host, 192.168.250.100, generated a burst of nineteen-plus anonymous-logon SMB pipe requests against lsarpc and samr within a five-second window, consistent with an automated enumeration tool rather than manual interactive use.",
    "support": "supporting",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 9,
      "quoted_span": "NT AUTHORITY\\ANONYMOUS LOGON"
     },
     {
      "finding_index": 27,
      "quoted_span": "RecordNumber=1084266"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "text": "Before the anonymous sweep, 192.168.250.100 had connected using its own machine account (WE8105DESK$) to the browser named pipe, which is routine SMB browsing-service traffic and not inherently suspicious.",
    "support": "counter",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 0,
      "quoted_span": "WE8105DESK$"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "text": "Every 5145 event's Access Check Results field is blank, meaning these logs record requested pipe access, not a confirmed grant, service creation, or payload execution -- the escalation pattern is suggestive but not proof of successful malicious action.",
    "support": "counter",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 7,
      "quoted_span": "Access Check Results:"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "text": "Both source IPs involved, 192.168.2.50 and 192.168.250.100, are internal RFC1918 addresses rather than externally attributed infrastructure, so this cannot be pinned on an outside attacker from this evidence alone.",
    "support": "counter",
    "citations": [
     {
      "finding_index": 3,
      "quoted_span": "192.168.2.50"
     },
     {
      "finding_index": 9,
      "quoted_span": "192.168.250.100"
     }
    ]
   }
  ],
  "counter_evidence_searched": true,
  "confidence": 0.66,
  "benign_explanation_considered": "The most plausible innocent reading is routine IT administration: an admin using the Administrator account to run remote management tasks that naturally touch svcctl/winreg/samr, with the anonymous-logon burst from the second host being an authorized scanner or SMB enumeration utility rather than an attacker. This survives partly -- the initial browser-pipe traffic from the machine account is clearly benign -- but a null anonymous session immediately followed by authenticated Administrator access on the identical source IP, then svcctl/winreg access, is not a pattern normal admin tooling or vulnerability scanners typically produce, so the benign explanation is weakened but not conclusively ruled out by log data alone."
 },
